  Here's my reasoning.
  Everything below the signal layer is an amplifier

  Orchestration, risk control, execution, the webapp — none of them create edge. They plumb,
  size, and route whatever the signal produces. If the signal has no edge, that stack is a
  very well-engineered machine for losing money at scale, plus a nice UI to watch it happen.
  Your own memory note says single-stock AUC is structurally sub-0.5. That's not a footnote —
  that's the central open question of the project, and it's still unanswered.

  The cardinal rule here: prove the edge cheaply before you build the machine around it.
  Building execution first inverts that, and it creates a nasty sunk-cost trap — once you've
  spent weeks on IBKR integration and a webapp, you'll be psychologically pushed to deploy a
  signal you'd otherwise reject.

  Cross-sectional ranking is the test

  This is the part that makes the decision easy: cross-sectional ranking isn't just "path 1, a  signal improvement." It's the natural payoff of the pooled model you just built (commit
  38f3acd), and it's precisely the framing under which equity ML edge usually lives. Absolute
  "will AAPL go up 5%" is dominated by market beta and is genuinely hard. "Will AAPL
  outperform the other 200 names this week" cancels the common market factor and is where
  relative alpha actually shows up — which is also why your single-name AUC looks structurally  poor. So path 1 does double duty: it's both the most promising signal direction and the
  definitive test of whether you have anything worth building on.

  So the sequencing is:

  1. Convert the pooled model output to a cross-sectional rank each day (rank the probs within  each date, long top decile / short bottom decile, or trade the rank directly).
  2. Backtest on rank-based metrics, not accuracy — rank IC (Spearman of predicted rank vs.
  realized forward-return rank) and long-short spread Sharpe. Watch the leakage risk your
  memory already flags — rank within date only, never pooling future dates into the
  normalization.
  3. Pre-commit a go/no-go number. Something like: rank IC > ~0.03 stable across folds,
  long-short Sharpe > ~1 after costs. Decide the threshold before you see the result.

  Then, and only then, build — as a thin vertical slice

  If it clears the gate, don't build the layers horizontally (finish all of orchestration,
  then all of risk, then all of execution). Build one thin end-to-end slice: today's ranks → a  dead-simple risk rule (fixed position count, gross cap) → IBKR paper for a handful of
  names. That surfaces the real integration pain (IBKR auth, order lifecycle, reconciliation)
  early, on a small surface, while everything is still cheap to change. The webapp and the
  natural-language control layer are the last thing — they're a presentation skin over a
  system that has to earn trust first.

  The one honest counter-argument: infrastructure is "known-good" work you'll need regardless,  so it feels productive to start now. True — but it's productive in the wrong order.
  Plumbing you build around an unvalidated signal often gets rebuilt once you learn what the
  signal actually needs (rebalance frequency, number of names, turnover, borrow constraints
  for the shorts). Let the validated ranking define those requirements.




