● Yes — and it's genuinely worth doing. What you're describing is a forward (paper) test, and
  it's the single most trustworthy validation you have, for one specific reason: it's the only
  data your model cannot have leaked from. Your backtest can be silently contaminated by
  lookahead (the leakage risk your memory already flags); a forward log physically can't be,  because the future hadn't happened when you wrote the prediction down. So it's the perfect
  complement to the walk-forward backtest — not a replacement.
  A few things you have to get right, though, or the table will lie to you:

  1. Your label is 5-day, so "check yesterday against reality" doesn't quite work

  The prediction you made yesterday isn't judged by yesterday→today; it's judged by the 5-day
  forward return, which isn't known for another 5 trading days. So the table has two clocks:
  - Prediction rows get written today (date, ticker, predicted prob, cross-sectional rank,
  decile/side, model version).
  - Labels mature on a lag — 5 trading days later you come back and fill in the realized
  return + realized rank.

  So on any given day you're writing new predictions and settling ones from a week ago. That's  fine, just design for it.

  2. Append-only + point-in-time, or it's worthless

  Two rules that matter more than they sound:
  - Never overwrite a prediction row. Once written, it's immutable. The whole value is that it  was recorded before the outcome.
  - Snapshot the data as it was known that day. Do not re-pull history later to compute the
  "actual." Vendors restate — splits, dividend adjustments, restatements — and if you
  re-fetch, past prices retroactively change and quietly poison your record. Store the
  realized return computed from data pulled at settlement time, and stamp it.

  Also stamp model version on every row, so when you retrain you don't blend two models' track  records together.

  3. Metrics: rank-based, and be patient

  Log daily rank IC (Spearman of predicted rank vs. realized-return rank across the
  cross-section) and the long-short decile spread, then accumulate cumulative return / hit
  rate / Sharpe. Watch out that consecutive days' predictions overlap (5-day horizon = 5
  overlapping books), so daily P&L isn't independent — don't over-interpret early Sharpe.

  The honest catch: forward testing is slow. Meaningful signal takes months of accumulation,
  and the first few weeks tell you almost nothing. That's exactly why you run it in parallel
  with the backtest — backtest for fast iteration and statistical power, forward log for
  ground truth and leakage detection.

  Why this is a good thing to build now

  This forward-logging harness is the cheapest, lowest-risk piece of the next layer — it's
  literally the logging/attribution backbone your eventual IBKR paper trading needs anyway. So  it fits the sequencing from before: build the ranking, wire it into an append-only daily
  prediction log with delayed settlement, and let it accumulate while you keep iterating on
  the backtest. It's dual-use — validation today, infrastructure later.

  One thing to decide before you build it: do you want this to run itself daily (a scheduled
  job that predicts + settles + updates the table with no input from you), or a script you run  manually? That choice drives the design — automated needs point-in-time data capture and
  idempotent settlement; manual can be looser. Which are you picturing?
